+<h2 id="exec">1. Executive Summary</h2>
+<p>The system is a custom web application for managing two sales modes: batch-based pre-orders and ready stock. It is intentionally not a marketplace. The business controls when a pre-order opens/closes, collects customer orders, procures from suppliers, receives stock, and fulfils orders through booth pickup or shipping.</p>
+<p><strong>Core principle:</strong> keep the customer experience simple while giving the small internal team strong operational control over orders, payment verification, inventory, fulfilment, shipping, pickup and audit history.</p>
+<p>The target operating model is a small business with approximately three staff and one physical fulfilment location. Customers are guests and do not need accounts.</p>
+
+<h2 id="vision">2. Product Vision & Goals</h2>
+<p>Create a focused order and fulfilment system with enough automation to eliminate spreadsheet-style operational work, without introducing marketplace complexity.</p>
+
+<h4>Goals</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Run time-bound pre-order batches.</li>
+  <li>Sell physically available ready stock.</li>
+  <li>Track customer commitments separately from physical inventory.</li>
+  <li>Support full payment and DP/partial payment.</li>
+  <li>Support manual bank-transfer verification.</li>
+  <li>Support booth pickup using QR scanning from a phone camera.</li>
+  <li>Support JNE shipping and separate shipping charges.</li>
+  <li>Generate printer-friendly shipping stickers.</li>
+  <li>Provide tracking numbers and shipment status.</li>
+  <li>Send useful transactional emails without requiring immediate delivery for every event.</li>
+  <li>Maintain a complete audit log.</li>
+  <li>Provide an order timeline.</li>
+  <li>Work as a responsive web app/PWA instead of a native mobile application.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Non-goals</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Marketplace functionality.</li>
+  <li>Customer accounts/passwords.</li>
+  <li>Integrated payment gateway in MVP.</li>
+  <li>Automatic supplier purchasing.</li>
+  <li>Complex warehouse management.</li>
+  <li>Automatic refunds in MVP.</li>
+  <li>Native iOS/Android apps.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="principles">3. Product Principles</h2>
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>Batch-first:</strong> pre-orders are temporary campaigns rather than permanently open listings.</li>
+  <li><strong>Guest-first:</strong> customers should not need accounts.</li>
+  <li><strong>Manual where appropriate:</strong> payment verification and exceptional refund decisions remain admin-controlled.</li>
+  <li><strong>Inventory is explicit:</strong> physical stock, reserved quantity and surplus stock must be distinguishable.</li>
+  <li><strong>Backend is the source of truth:</strong> frontend actions cannot bypass business rules.</li>
+  <li><strong>Audit everything important:</strong> manual operations must be traceable.</li>
+  <li><strong>Mobile-first operations:</strong> staff should be able to run critical workflows from a phone.</li>
+  <li><strong>Do not overbuild:</strong> avoid marketplace-style complexity that does not support the actual business model.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h2 id="users">4. Users & Roles</h2>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Role</th><th>Responsibilities</th><th>Access</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Customer / Guest</td><td>View products/batches, place orders, submit payment information, view order progress, choose fulfilment, present pickup QR.</td><td>Public storefront and secure order access.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Admin / Store Staff</td><td>Manage products, batches, orders, payments, inventory, shipping and pickup.</td><td>Authenticated admin dashboard.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>System</td><td>Calculates statuses, queues emails, validates QR tokens, records timestamps and audit events.</td><td>Backend only.</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h2 id="sales">5. Sales Models</h2>
+
+<h3>5.1 Pre-Order</h3>
+<p>A pre-order product is available only while its batch is open. Customers place orders during that window. After closure, the business decides how to fulfil supplier MOQ and procurement requirements.</p>
+
+<h4>MOQ Example</h4>
+<pre>Customer orders:      20
+Supplier MOQ:         24
+Procured:             24
+Customer reserved:    20
+Surplus ready stock:   4</pre>
+<p>The system must therefore distinguish customer-reserved quantity from unallocated/ready-stock quantity.</p>
+
+<h3>5.2 Ready Stock</h3>
+<p>Ready-stock products are sold from physical on-hand inventory. A product may transition from being part of a pre-order batch into ready stock when supplier quantity exceeds customer commitments.</p>
+
+<h2 id="e2e">6. End-to-End Business Flow</h2>
+
+<h4>PRE-ORDER</h4>
+<pre>Batch opens
+→ Customer submits order + payment information
+→ Payment pending
+→ Admin verifies payment
+→ Customer quantity reserved
+→ Batch closes
+→ Admin decides procurement quantity / MOQ
+→ Supplier sends goods
+→ Business receives stock
+→ Customer order becomes fulfilment-ready
+→ If DP: balance requested and verified
+→ Pickup OR shipping
+→ Completed</pre>
+
+<h4>READY STOCK</h4>
+<pre>Product has on-hand available quantity
+→ Customer orders + submits payment
+→ Admin verifies payment
+→ Stock reserved
+→ Pickup OR shipping
+→ Completed</pre>
+
+<h2 id="ordering">7. Customer Ordering</h2>
+
+<h3>7.1 Guest Checkout</h3>
+<p>No customer account is required. The minimum identity/contact model is:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>Customer name</li>
+  <li>Phone number</li>
+  <li>Email address</li>
+  <li>Fulfilment information</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Phone number is the primary customer contact/lookup value. Secure order tokens can be delivered through the checkout success page and email.</p>
+
+<h3>7.2 Order + Payment Submission</h3>
+<div class="callout callout-new">
+  <strong><span class="badge badge-new">CHANGED v1.3</span></strong> Order creation and initial payment submission are <strong>one atomic flow</strong>. The customer does not create an order and then attach payment later.
+</div>
+<p>The order is only persisted after:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>Customer identity (name, phone, email)</li>
+  <li>Items/variants/quantities</li>
+  <li>Payment type (DP or FULL)</li>
+  <li><strong>Payment proof / transfer reference</strong> (uploaded in the same form)</li>
+  <li>Fulfilment method</li>
+</ul>
+<p>There is no "order created but payment pending submission" state. If the form is not completed, no order exists.</p>
+
+<h4>Order Data</h4>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Order ID</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Customer name</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Phone</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Email</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Items / variants / quantities</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Sales mode</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Batch</td><td>For pre-orders</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Payment type</td><td>DP or FULL</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Payment submission</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Fulfilment method</td><td>Yes or configured later</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Shipping information</td><td>For shipping orders</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h2 id="payment">8. Payment</h2>
+
+<h3>8.1 MVP</h3>
+<p>Payment is handled through bank transfer. The customer submits payment information/proof as part of the order flow. Admin manually checks the bank transfer and verifies or rejects it.</p>
+
+<h3>8.2 Payment Types</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>FULL:</strong> applicable merchandise/payment amount is paid upfront.</li>
+  <li><strong>DP:</strong> customer pays a deposit and the remaining amount is paid after stock is ready.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>DP is a single global rule — <strong>50% of order total</strong> — applied system-wide, not configurable per batch or per product. Which payment type(s) are offered at checkout is controlled per batch (§10.1); the DP amount itself is not.</p>
+
+<h3>8.3 Verification</h3>
+<p>Every payment must have an explicit verification state and an admin actor.</p>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Data</th><th>Description</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Amount</td><td>Amount submitted/verified.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Submitted at</td><td>Timestamp.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Status</td><td>Pending / Verified / Rejected.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Verified by</td><td>Admin user.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Verified at</td><td>Timestamp.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Rejection reason</td><td>Required when rejected.</td></tr>
+</table>
+<p><strong>Important:</strong> a submitted transfer must never automatically become a verified payment simply because the form was submitted.</p>
+<p>Uploaded payment proof files are retained for <strong>30 days</strong> after the related order reaches a completed fulfilment state (<code>SHIPPED</code> or <code>PICKED_UP</code>), then purged.</p>
+
+<h2 id="states">9. Order State Machine</h2>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>PAYMENT_PENDING</code></td><td>Order/payment submitted but payment has not been verified.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>PAYMENT_VERIFIED</code></td><td>Initial payment has been verified.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>RESERVED</code></td><td>Customer quantity is committed.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>AWAITING_STOCK</code></td><td>Pre-order is confirmed but physical stock has not arrived.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>BALANCE_DUE</code></td><td>DP order has an outstanding balance.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>READY_FOR_FULFILMENT</code></td><td>Required payment and stock conditions are satisfied.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>READY_FOR_PICKUP</code></td><td>Order is prepared for booth pickup.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>PICKED_UP</code></td><td>Staff confirmed pickup.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>READY_TO_SHIP</code></td><td>Order is packed and ready for courier handover.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>SHIPPED</code></td><td>Tracking number recorded and parcel dispatched/handover completed.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>COMPLETED</code></td><td>Fulfilment lifecycle is complete.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>CANCELLED</code></td><td>Order cancelled.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>REFUND_REQUIRED</code></td><td>Admin must manually contact/process refund.</td></tr>
+</table>
+<p><strong>Resolved (v1.2):</strong> <code>AWAITING_STOCK</code> and <code>BALANCE_DUE</code> are not simultaneous. A DP pre-order stays in <code>AWAITING_STOCK</code> until stock is physically received; only then does it move to <code>BALANCE_DUE</code> (if unpaid balance remains) or directly to <code>READY_FOR_FULFILMENT</code> (if already paid in full).</p>
+
+<h2 id="batch">10. Batch Management</h2>
+
+<h3>10.1 Batch Data</h3>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Batch ID</td><td>Unique identifier.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Name</td><td>Customer-facing batch name.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Open date/time</td><td>Opening time.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Close date/time</td><td>Scheduled closing time.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>MOQ</td><td>Supplier minimum order quantity.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Ordered quantity</td><td>Total confirmed customer quantity.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Procured quantity</td><td>Quantity actually ordered from supplier.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Status</td><td>Draft, Open, Closed, Procurement, Awaiting Stock, Received, Fulfilment, Completed.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Allowed payment types</td><td>DP only, FULL only, or both — set by admin per batch.</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>10.2 Lifecycle</h3>
+<pre>DRAFT → OPEN → CLOSED → PROCUREMENT → AWAITING_STOCK → RECEIVED → FULFILMENT → COMPLETED</pre>
+<p>Admin can manually close a batch before the scheduled closing time.</p>
+
+<h3>10.3 MOQ Decision</h3>
+<p>The system should show ordered quantity versus MOQ, but the final procurement decision remains an admin action. The system must not automatically cancel a batch simply because it is below MOQ.</p>
+
+<h2 id="inventory">11. Inventory & Reservation</h2>
+
+<h3>11.1 Definitions</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>On-hand:</strong> physical stock currently received by the business.</li>
+  <li><strong>Reserved:</strong> physical quantity committed to confirmed customer orders.</li>
+  <li><strong>Available:</strong> on-hand minus reserved quantity.</li>
+  <li><strong>Incoming:</strong> quantity ordered from supplier but not physically received.</li>
+  <li><strong>Surplus:</strong> received quantity remaining after customer commitments are allocated.</li>
+</ul>
+<pre>Available = On-hand − Reserved</pre>
+
+<h3>11.2 Rules</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li>Pre-order commitments are tracked even before physical stock exists.</li>
+  <li>When supplier stock is received, physical inventory is increased.</li>
+  <li>Customer commitments are allocated first.</li>
+  <li>Any excess quantity becomes available ready stock.</li>
+  <li>Inventory adjustments require a reason and audit event.</li>
+  <li>The system must prevent accidental negative available stock.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="ready">12. Ready Stock</h2>
+<p>Ready stock is inventory that can be sold immediately. Surplus from an MOQ procurement can be converted into ready stock after all eligible customer reservations are accounted for.</p>
+<p>Admin should explicitly control whether newly created surplus is immediately published for sale or kept unpublished.</p>
+
+<h2 id="fulfilment">13. Fulfilment</h2>
+
+<h3>13.1 Methods</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li>Booth pickup</li>
+  <li>Shipping</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>13.2 Booth Pickup</h3>
+<p>Eligible customers receive a QR linked to their order. Staff scans it from a phone using the web/PWA camera.</p>
+<p>The scan result should show:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>Order ID</li>
+  <li>Customer name</li>
+  <li>Masked phone number</li>
+  <li>Items and quantities</li>
+  <li>Payment status</li>
+  <li>Pickup eligibility</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Staff must explicitly confirm the pickup. The system then records the staff member and timestamp.</p>
+
+<h3>13.3 QR Behaviour</h3>
+<p>The QR should contain a random, unguessable token rather than sensitive customer information. The token is validated server-side.</p>
+<p>It does not need to be physically destroyed after one scan. Instead, the backend makes the order state authoritative:</p>
+<pre>VALID QR + READY_FOR_PICKUP
+→ show order
+→ staff confirms pickup
+→ PICKED_UP
+
+Same QR after pickup
+→ backend detects PICKED_UP
+→ deny second fulfilment
+→ show "Already picked up"</pre>
+<p>QR scanning is staff-authenticated only. If the scanning session is not an authenticated staff session, the app shows a generic "Login required" message and nothing else.</p>
+
+<h2 id="scanner">14. QR / Barcode Scanner</h2>
+<p>The scanner is a browser/PWA feature. There is no requirement for a native app.</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>Request camera permission when scanning starts.</li>
+  <li>Support modern mobile browsers.</li>
+  <li>Validate every scan against the backend.</li>
+  <li>Provide manual Order ID fallback.</li>
+  <li>Show clear success/error states.</li>
+  <li>Do not put sensitive customer information into the QR.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>QR is recommended for customer pickup. Standard barcodes can additionally be supported later for internal product/inventory scanning.</p>
+
+<h2 id="shipping">15. Shipping</h2>
+
+<h3>15.1 Courier</h3>
+<p>JNE is the initial courier. The architecture should isolate courier integration behind a shipping service so another courier can be added later (we will use api.co.id).</p>
+
+<h3>15.2 Cost Model</h3>
+<pre>Merchandise subtotal
++ shipping cost
+= customer payable amount</pre>
+<p>The system can use a shipping-cost API where available. The API integration should not make the entire order system dependent on a single provider being available.</p>
+
+<h3>15.3 Shipping Flow</h3>
+<pre>Shipping selected
+→ Address entered
+→ Shipping option/rate calculated
+→ Shipping cost stored
+→ Payment verified
+→ Order ready to ship
+→ Admin packs
+→ Shipping sticker generated
+→ Courier handover
+→ Tracking number recorded
+→ SHIPPED
+→ Customer notified</pre>
+
+<h3>15.4 Shipping Sticker</h3>
+<p>The system shall provide a print-friendly shipping sticker containing:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>Recipient name</li>
+  <li>Recipient phone</li>
+  <li>Delivery address</li>
+  <li>Courier/service</li>
+  <li>Tracking number, once available</li>
+  <li>Order ID</li>
+  <li>Optional internal QR/barcode</li>
+</ul>
+<p>The sticker must have a dedicated print CSS/layout so staff does not need to manually format the label.</p>
+
+<h2 id="customer-page">16. Customer Order Page</h2>
+<p>The customer should be able to view the order without creating an account.</p>
+
+<h4>Display</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Order ID</li>
+  <li>Items and quantities</li>
+  <li>Merchandise subtotal</li>
+  <li>Shipping cost if applicable</li>
+  <li>Amount paid</li>
+  <li>Balance due</li>
+  <li>Payment status</li>
+  <li>Order/batch progress</li>
+  <li>Fulfilment method</li>
+  <li>Pickup QR when applicable</li>
+  <li>Courier/tracking information when shipped</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>16.1 Secure Order Access</h3>
+<div class="callout callout-new">
+  <strong><span class="badge badge-new">CHANGED v1.3</span></strong> The order URL contains a <strong>high-entropy random token</strong> (minimum 32 characters, URL-safe base64). It is not guessable.
+</div>
+<ul>
+  <li>The token is generated server-side and stored hashed in the database.</li>
+  <li>The token alone grants read-only access to the order page.</li>
+  <li><strong>Rate limiting:</strong> The endpoint accepts maximum 10 requests per minute per IP for token-based access.</li>
+  <li><strong>No expiration:</strong> The token does not expire, but can be revoked by admin if fraud is suspected.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>16.2 Order Access Recovery</h3>
+<p>If a guest loses their link, they can recover access by:</p>
+<ol>
+  <li>Entering their <strong>phone number</strong> on the lookup page.</li>
+  <li>The system cross-checks the phone against the <strong>email address</strong> on file for that order.</li>
+  <li>If both match, the existing order page (with the same token URL) is displayed.</li>
+  <li><strong>No new email, no new token, no SMS</strong> is sent. The customer simply re-opens the link they already had.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h3>16.3 Order Timeline</h3>
+<pre>Order created
+✓ Payment submitted
+✓ Payment verified
+✓ Order reserved
+✓ Stock received
+✓ Balance paid
+✓ Ready for pickup
+→ Picked up</pre>
+<p>The exact timeline entries shown depend on the order's path.</p>
+
+<h2 id="email">17. Email Notification Strategy</h2>
+<div class="callout callout-new">
+  <strong><span class="badge badge-new">CHANGED v1.3</span></strong> Email is <strong>transactional and minimal</strong>. Because the order page is the source of truth, emails are short nudges containing only the event type and a single link.
+</div>
+
+<h3>17.1 Core Email Events (4 emails only)</h3>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Event</th><th>Customer Email</th><th>Priority</th><th>Content</th></tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Order confirmed + payment verified</strong></td>
+    <td>Yes</td>
+    <td><span class="badge badge-p1">P1</span></td>
+    <td><em>"Order #[ID] confirmed. Track here: [link]"</em><br><small>Sent <strong>after</strong> admin verifies payment. Combines order confirmation and payment verification into one email.</small></td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Payment rejected</strong></td>
+    <td>Yes</td>
+    <td><span class="badge badge-p0">P0</span></td>
+    <td><em>"Action required for order #[ID]: [link]"</em></td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Balance due</strong></td>
+    <td>Yes</td>
+    <td><span class="badge badge-p0">P0</span></td>
+    <td><em>"Order #[ID] is ready. Pay balance: [link]"</em></td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Ready for fulfilment</strong></td>
+    <td>Yes</td>
+    <td><span class="badge badge-p1">P1</span></td>
+    <td><em>"Order #[ID] is ready: [link]"</em><br><small>Covers both pickup QR and shipping tracking.</small></td>
+  </tr>
+</table>
+
+<h4>Skipped Events (v1.3)</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Payment verified as standalone — merged into "Order confirmed"</li>
+  <li>Shipped — customer sees tracking on the order page</li>
+  <li>Picked up — customer was physically present</li>
+  <li>Completed — redundant; order page shows final state</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>17.2 Email Body Rules</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>One link only.</strong> No order details, no tables, no images in the email body.</li>
+  <li>Subject line contains order ID and action keyword.</li>
+  <li>Footer: business name only. No unsubscribe needed (transactional).</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>17.3 P0 Queue-Jumping</h3>
+<p>P0 emails (payment rejected, balance due) jump ahead of P1 in the queue. If the queue is backed up, P1 emails wait; P0 emails send immediately.</p>
+
+<h2 id="push">17a. Push Notification Backup</h2>
+<div class="callout callout-new">
+  <strong><span class="badge badge-new">NEW v1.3</span></strong> Web Push is a <strong>$0 backup channel</strong> that bypasses email caps and spam filters.
+</div>
+
+<h3>17a.1 Subscription</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li>Triggered on the order page: browser asks <em>"Allow notifications?"</em></li>
+  <li>If accepted, the browser's push subscription endpoint is stored in the database, linked to the <strong>order ID</strong>.</li>
+  <li><strong>No customer account required.</strong> The subscription belongs to the order, not a user profile.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>17a.2 Push Events</h3>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Event</th><th>Push?</th><th>Body</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Payment rejected</td><td>Yes</td><td><em>"Order #[ID]: Action required"</em></td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Balance due</td><td>Yes</td><td><em>"Order #[ID]: Balance due. Tap to pay."</em></td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Ready for fulfilment</td><td>Yes</td><td><em>"Order #[ID]: Ready for pickup/shipping."</em></td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Order confirmed</td><td>No</td><td>Not needed; customer discovers this via email or by checking the page</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>17a.3 WhatsApp Manual Fallback</h3>
+<p>If email and push both fail (or for customers who opt out), staff can export phone numbers from the admin dashboard and send template WhatsApp messages manually. This is acknowledged as a valid operational fallback for a 3-person team.</p>
+
+<h2 id="admin">18. Admin Dashboard</h2>
+
+<h3>18.1 Dashboard Widgets</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li>Payment verification queue</li>
+  <li>DP balances due</li>
+  <li>Active batches</li>
+  <li>Batches below/at/above MOQ</li>
+  <li>Orders awaiting stock</li>
+  <li>Orders ready for pickup</li>
+  <li>Orders ready to ship</li>
+  <li>Shipments missing tracking numbers</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>18.2 Order Search/Filter</h3>
+<p>Search/filter by order ID, customer name, phone, product, batch, payment status, fulfilment method, order status and date.</p>
+
+<h3>18.3 Admin Actions</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li>Create/edit products.</li>
+  <li>Open/close batches.</li>
+  <li>Verify/reject payments.</li>
+  <li>Record supplier receipt.</li>
+  <li>Allocate/release inventory.</li>
+  <li>Set fulfilment method where permitted.</li>
+  <li>Calculate/override shipping cost with audit reason.</li>
+  <li>Generate/print shipping label.</li>
+  <li>Record tracking number.</li>
+  <li>Scan/confirm pickup.</li>
+  <li>Trigger eligible customer emails.</li>
+  <li>Review audit history.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>18.4 Admin Roles & Permissions</h3>
+<p>Access is granted per action, not through a single fixed role. Each admin account has an independent set of permission toggles. All three staff can be granted full access on day one, but the model supports narrowing individual permissions later without any rebuild.</p>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Permission</th><th>Covers</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Verify payments</td><td>Verify/reject submitted payments (DP and full).</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Scan / confirm pickup</td><td>Use the QR scanner, confirm booth pickup.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Manage products & batches</td><td>Create/edit products, open/close batches, set batch payment-type rules.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Adjust inventory</td><td>Record supplier receipt, allocate/release stock, manual adjustments.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Manage shipping</td><td>Pack orders, override shipping cost, generate labels, record tracking.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>View audit log</td><td>Access the full audit history.</td></tr>
+</table>
+<p>The dashboard itself is read-only for everyone regardless of permissions — every admin can see all widgets, queues and order data. Actions the admin does not have permission for appear disabled, not hidden.</p>
+
+<h2 id="security">19. Security</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>HTTPS everywhere.</li>
+  <li>Authenticated admin area.</li>
+  <li>Server-side authorization and role checks.</li>
+  <li>Secure session cookies.</li>
+  <li>Strong/random customer order access tokens.</li>
+  <li>Unpredictable QR tokens.</li>
+  <li>Rate limiting on public lookup and scan endpoints.</li>
+  <li>CSRF protection where applicable.</li>
+  <li>Input validation and output encoding.</li>
+  <li>Upload validation for payment proofs.</li>
+  <li>Idempotency/duplicate-submission protection for order creation, implemented via a one-time submission token issued with the order form plus a disabled-on-click submit button.</li>
+  <li>Do not expose bank/payment credentials unnecessarily in logs.</li>
+  <li>Audit security-sensitive actions.</li>
+  <li>Admin MFA should be supported where feasible.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="audit">20. Audit Log</h2>
+<p>The system shall maintain a full audit trail for important administrative actions.</p>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Event</th><th>Example</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Order created</td><td>Customer created order.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Payment verified</td><td>Admin verified transfer amount.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Payment rejected</td><td>Admin rejected with reason.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Status changed</td><td><code>READY_TO_SHIP</code> → <code>SHIPPED</code>.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Inventory received</td><td>Supplier receipt added.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Inventory adjusted</td><td>Admin changed quantity with reason.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Shipping cost changed</td><td>Admin override recorded.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Tracking added</td><td>Tracking number recorded.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Pickup confirmed</td><td>Staff confirmed via QR.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Refund state changed</td><td>Order marked refund required.</td></tr>
+</table>
+<p>Each event should include actor, timestamp, entity type/ID, action and before/after values when relevant.</p>
+
