+<h2 id="email-arch">24. Email Architecture</h2>
+<div class="callout callout-new">
+  <strong><span class="badge badge-new">CHANGED v1.3</span></strong> Resend is the <strong>sole email provider</strong> for MVP. No custom domain required.
+</div>
+
+<h3>24.1 Provider</h3>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Resend Free Tier</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Monthly limit</td><td>3,000 emails</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Daily hard cap</td><td>100 emails/day</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Sender address</td><td><code>yourstore@resend.dev</code> (no custom domain)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Branding</td><td>None in email body</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Setup</td><td>Email verification only; no DNS</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h3>24.2 Queue Worker Logic</h3>
+<pre>Order event → Create email job (P0 or P1)
+            → Queue
+            → Worker checks daily counter
+            → Resend API
+            → Delivery status → Email log</pre>
+
+<h4>Cap Rules</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>P0 (payment rejected, balance due):</strong> Send immediately. If Resend returns 429 (daily limit hit), queue for next day 00:01. Never dropped.</li>
+  <li><strong>P1 (order confirmed, ready for fulfilment):</strong> Send only if today's count &lt; 90. If cap hit, defer to next day.</li>
+  <li><strong>Balance due batching:</strong> If &gt;80 DP orders become balance-due simultaneously, send in batches of 80/day, ordered by payment verification time (earliest first). Remainder sends the next day.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>24.3 Graceful Degradation</h3>
+<p>Critical business state never depends on email delivery. An order can become <code>READY_FOR_FULFILMENT</code> even if the email provider temporarily fails. The admin dashboard and customer order page remain the source of truth.</p>
+
+<h3>24.4 Future Upgrade Path</h3>
+<p>When revenue justifies it:</p>
+<ol>
+  <li>Purchase custom domain (~$10/year)</li>
+  <li>Verify domain in Resend</li>
+  <li>Switch sender to <code>noreply@yourstore.com</code></li>
+  <li>Optionally upgrade to Resend paid ($20/mo, 50K emails, no daily cap)</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h2 id="pwa">25. PWA & Camera Architecture</h2>
+<p>The application should be a responsive web app enhanced with PWA capabilities. Staff can install it to their phone home screen and use the camera scanner without installing a native app.</p>
+
+<h4>Scanner Architecture</h4>
+<pre>Staff opens PWA scanner
+→ Browser requests camera permission
+→ Camera detects QR
+→ Frontend receives token
+→ Backend validates token + order state
+→ Order details returned
+→ Staff confirms pickup
+→ Backend changes order state
+→ Audit event created</pre>
+
+<p>The PWA may cache static application assets, but transaction/order data must remain server-authoritative.</p>
+
+<h2 id="edge">26. Edge Cases & Business Rules</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Duplicate order submission must not create accidental duplicate orders.</li>
+  <li>Payment can be rejected and resubmitted according to configured rules.</li>
+  <li>DP orders remain balance-due until remaining payment is verified.</li>
+  <li>Supplier delivers fewer units than procured: orders are fulfilled in order of payment verification time (earliest-verified first); orders that cannot be fulfilled from the shortfall are cancelled manually by admin.</li>
+  <li>Supplier delivers more units: excess becomes ready stock.</li>
+  <li>Batch closes below MOQ: admin decides whether to extend or procure MOQ.</li>
+  <li>Customer cancellation is permitted any time before the order reaches <code>SHIPPED</code> (shipping orders) or <code>PICKED_UP</code> (pickup orders); refund decisions remain manual per business policy.</li>
+  <li>Duplicate order submission is prevented via a one-time submission token plus a disabled-on-click submit button — this only blocks accidental double-submits. A customer intentionally placing a second, separate order is expected and allowed.</li>
+  <li>Shipping cost can be manually corrected, but the override must be audited.</li>
+  <li>Courier API failure must not prevent manual shipment processing.</li>
+  <li>Email failure must not roll back order state.</li>
+  <li>Already-picked-up QR must never release goods a second time.</li>
+  <li>Invalid QR must produce a safe error without revealing customer information.</li>
+  <li>Inventory adjustments must not silently create negative stock.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="sec-detail">27. Security & Privacy Detail</h2>
+
+<h3>Order Link Security</h3>
+<div class="callout callout-new">
+  <strong><span class="badge badge-new">CHANGED v1.3</span></strong> Order access tokens are <strong>cryptographically random</strong> (32+ bytes, base64url encoded).
+</div>
+<ul>
+  <li>Tokens are stored hashed (SHA-256) in the database; the raw token only exists in the customer's URL.</li>
+  <li>The order page exposes <strong>no sensitive data</strong> to unauthenticated requests beyond what the token authorizes.</li>
+  <li>Brute-force protection: 10 failed token lookups per IP per minute triggers a temporary block.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>QR Security</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li>QR payload contains a <strong>random token only</strong> — no customer name, phone, or order details in the barcode itself.</li>
+  <li>Server validates token + order state before returning any data.</li>
+  <li>Scanning requires an authenticated staff session. Unauthenticated scans return only: <em>"Login required."</em></li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>Admin Security</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li>Strong authentication.</li>
+  <li>Session timeout.</li>
+  <li>Server-side authorization.</li>
+  <li>MFA where feasible.</li>
+  <li>Audit logs that cannot be edited through the normal UI.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="mvp">28. Recommended MVP Scope</h2>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Capability</th><th>MVP</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Product catalogue</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Pre-order batch management</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Ready stock</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Guest checkout</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Manual bank transfer</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>DP / full payment</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Inventory and reservation</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Pickup</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>QR pickup scanner</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>JNE shipping</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Shipping label</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Tracking number</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Email queue</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Web push backup</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Full audit log</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Customer accounts</td><td>No</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Payment gateway</td><td>No</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Automatic refunds</td><td>No</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Multiple couriers</td><td>Future</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Marketplace functionality</td><td>No</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<h2 id="future">29. Future Enhancements</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Additional couriers.</li>
+  <li>Automatic payment reconciliation.</li>
+  <li>Automatic refunds.</li>
+  <li>WhatsApp notifications (automated via API).</li>
+  <li>Customer self-service changes.</li>
+  <li>Inventory barcode scanning.</li>
+  <li>Supplier management.</li>
+  <li>Profitability/reporting dashboard.</li>
+  <li>MOQ forecasting.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="arch">30. Suggested Architecture</h2>
+<pre>Customer / Staff Browser
+        ↓
+Responsive Web App / PWA
+        ↓
+Backend API
+        ├── Auth & Authorization
+        ├── Product & Batch
+        ├── Order
+        ├── Payment Verification
+        ├── Inventory
+        ├── Fulfilment
+        ├── QR Token
+        ├── Shipping Adapter
+        ├── Email Queue
+        ├── Push Notification
+        └── Audit Log
+        ↓
+Relational Database</pre>
+
+<p>The browser handles camera capture and QR/barcode decoding. The backend validates the decoded value and performs all authorization and business-state changes.</p>
+
+<h2 id="decisions">31. Key Architectural Decisions</h2>
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>PWA instead of native:</strong> current staff use case does not justify maintaining iOS/Android apps.</li>
+  <li><strong>QR for pickup:</strong> flexible and suitable for secure order identification.</li>
+  <li><strong>Manual payment:</strong> avoids payment gateway cost and complexity initially.</li>
+  <li><strong>Shipping as a separate charge:</strong> keeps merchandise and logistics accounting clear.</li>
+  <li><strong>Email queue:</strong> allows delayed/batched sending and provider failover.</li>
+  <li><strong>No customer accounts:</strong> phone + secure order access is sufficient for the expected use.</li>
+  <li><strong>Audit log:</strong> critical because many operational decisions are manual.</li>
+  <li><strong>Workflow-driven model:</strong> batch/order lifecycle is more important than marketplace-style catalogue complexity.</li>
+  <li><strong>Resend free tier for MVP:</strong> $0 cost, no domain required, acceptable trade-offs for a small business starting out.</li>
+  <li><strong>Web push as backup:</strong> bypasses email caps and spam filters without requiring accounts or paid services.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h2 id="acceptance">32. Acceptance Criteria — Core Scenarios</h2>
+
+<h4>Scenario A — Full-payment pre-order</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Customer places guest order.</li>
+  <li>Initial payment is submitted in the same flow.</li>
+  <li>Order starts <code>PAYMENT_PENDING</code>.</li>
+  <li>Admin verifies payment.</li>
+  <li>System sends <strong>order confirmed</strong> email (P1).</li>
+  <li>Customer quantity becomes reserved.</li>
+  <li>Order waits for stock.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Scenario B — DP pre-order</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Customer selects DP.</li>
+  <li>DP is submitted and manually verified.</li>
+  <li>Order remains reserved/awaiting stock.</li>
+  <li>Stock is received.</li>
+  <li>System calculates balance.</li>
+  <li>Customer receives <strong>balance-due</strong> notification (P0 email + push).</li>
+  <li>Remaining payment is manually verified.</li>
+  <li>Order becomes fulfilment-ready.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Scenario C — MOQ surplus</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Admin sees customer quantity and MOQ.</li>
+  <li>Admin chooses to extend or procure MOQ.</li>
+  <li>Supplier quantity is recorded when received.</li>
+  <li>Customer commitments are allocated first.</li>
+  <li>Surplus becomes available ready stock.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Scenario D — Booth pickup</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Eligible order has pickup QR.</li>
+  <li>Staff opens scanner on phone.</li>
+  <li>Camera scans QR.</li>
+  <li>Backend validates token and order state.</li>
+  <li>Staff sees order details.</li>
+  <li>Staff confirms pickup.</li>
+  <li>Order becomes <code>PICKED_UP</code> and audit log is written.</li>
+  <li>Repeated scan cannot cause another fulfilment.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h4>Scenario E — Shipping</h4>
+<ul>
+  <li>Customer selects shipping.</li>
+  <li>Address is collected.</li>
+  <li>JNE rate is obtained from configured shipping integration where available.</li>
+  <li>Shipping cost is stored separately.</li>
+  <li>Admin packs order.</li>
+  <li>Shipping sticker is generated.</li>
+  <li>Tracking number is recorded.</li>
+  <li>Order becomes <code>SHIPPED</code>.</li>
+  <li>Customer sees tracking on order page.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="open">33. Open Decisions Before Implementation</h2>
+<ol>
+  <li>Exact DP percentage/amount. — <strong>Resolved v1.2:</strong> single global rule, 50% of order total.</li>
+  <li>Exact moment balance becomes due after stock is ready. — <strong>Resolved v1.2:</strong> immediately once stock is received.</li>
+  <li>Whether fulfilment method is selected at checkout or can be chosen later.</li>
+  <li>Exact JNE API/provider and available service levels.</li>
+  <li>Shipping label paper/printer format.</li>
+  <li>Email provider and operational sending limit. — <strong>Resolved v1.3:</strong> Resend free tier, 100/day hard cap.</li>
+  <li>How long unpaid/DP orders retain reservations. — <strong>Partially resolved v1.2:</strong> no automatic release — admin is notified via dashboard and follows up manually; no fixed timeout.</li>
+  <li>Cancellation/refund policy. — <strong>Partially resolved v1.2:</strong> cancellation window is before <code>SHIPPED</code>/<code>PICKED_UP</code>; refund decisions remain manual per business policy.</li>
+  <li>Whether batch extension automatically emails existing customers.</li>
+  <li>Whether surplus stock is auto-published or requires admin approval.</li>
+  <li>Exact admin role permissions for the three staff. — <strong>Resolved v1.2:</strong> per-action permission toggles, not fixed roles (§18.4).</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h2 id="metrics">34. Success Metrics</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Reduced manual spreadsheet/order tracking.</li>
+  <li>Lower fulfilment error rate.</li>
+  <li>Time required to verify and fulfil an order.</li>
+  <li>Inventory discrepancy rate.</li>
+  <li>Successful pickup QR verification rate.</li>
+  <li>Email delivery success rate.</li>
+  <li>Percentage of orders with complete audit history.</li>
+  <li>Time from stock receipt to fulfilment readiness.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2 id="final">35. Final Product Definition</h2>
+<p>This is not a marketplace. It is a controlled sales, inventory and fulfilment system for a small business.</p>
+<p>The customer experience stays simple: browse → order → pay → wait for stock if necessary → pickup or ship. The admin side handles the complexity of batches, MOQ, procurement, inventory allocation, payment verification and fulfilment.</p>
+<p>The key domain concept is the distinction between customer commitments and physical inventory. When a batch requires MOQ procurement, customer reservations are fulfilled first; excess supplier quantity can become ready stock. This lets the same system support both pre-order and ready-stock sales without turning into a marketplace.</p>
+<p>PWA is the recommended operational interface because staff can scan QR codes using their phone cameras directly from the browser, while the backend remains the authoritative source for order, payment, inventory and fulfilment state.</p>
+
